course: dictionary
lesson: 11995
title: Chapped
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Chapped skin or lips are dry, rough and cracked. You can often work out the meaning from the clues around it. «Her chapped lips stung in the icy wind.» Icy wind is cold, and stung tells you it hurts, so chapped must mean dry, rough and sore.
explain: Chapped is also the past form of the verb chap, which means to make skin crack. «The cold wind chapped my cheeks.» Put your meaning into the sentence: the wind cracked my cheeks and made them sore. It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Look for the things that help. «After the snow, his hands were red and chapped, so Dad gave him hand cream.» Cream helps dry skin, so the cream is a clue that chapped means dry and sore. Chapped lips and hands are common in winter.
fact: Chapped skin or lips are dry, rough, cracked and sore.
fact: Cold wind and dry air often make skin chapped, because they take the moisture out of it.
fact: Chapped is the past form of the verb chap: «The cold wind chapped my lips.»
fact: Lip balm, gloves and hand cream can help stop lips and hands from getting chapped.
fact: Writers use chapped hands or lips to show a cold, harsh place or a person who works hard outside.
exercise: «Her chapped lips stung in the icy wind.» What does chapped mean? | Covered in paint | Smooth and shiny | Warm and wet | *Dry, rough and cracked
exercise: Mum rubbed cream into her chapped hands. What might have helped them? | Sunshine on a beach | Muddy boots | *Hand cream | Ice in a cold drink
exercise: «The cold wind chapped my cheeks.» Which word is the past form of the verb chap? | *chapped | wind | cold | cheeks
test: «Sam forgot his gloves, so his fingers were chapped by the frosty morning.» What does chapped mean here? | Full of jam | Warmed up by the fire | *Dry and cracked from the cold | Painted bright red
test: Why might a story describe a farmer with chapped hands? | To show he is a famous singer | To show he has just won a prize | *To show a cold, harsh place and hard work | To show he is on holiday in the sun
exam: Which word could replace chapped in «My lips are chapped»? | Shiny and smooth | Sweet and sticky | Loud and fast | *Cracked and sore
